<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">


<!-- 
	
	
	The @Parameters annotation in TestNG is used to pass values from the testng.xml file to test methods or configuration methods.

It helps execute the same test method with different input values without hardcoding those values inside the Java code.
1. Basic syntax
Step 1: Define parameters in testng.xml



<suite name="ParameterSuite">

    <test name="LoginTest">

        <parameter name="username" value="admin"/>
        <parameter name="password" value="admin123"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="test.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>

    </test>

</suite>


Step 2: Receive parameters in the Java class


public class LoginTest {

    @Test
    @Parameters({"username", "password"})
    public void login(String username, String password) {

        System.out.println("Username: " + username);
        System.out.println("Password: " + password);

    }
}


public class LoginTest {

    @Test
    @Parameters({"username", "password"})
    public void login(String username, String password) {

        System.out.println("Username: " + username);
        System.out.println("Password: " + password);

    }
}


Here:
- name in XML identifies the parameter.
- value contains the value to be passed.
- @Parameters specifies which XML parameters should be received.
- Java



2. Passing different values to multiple XML <test> blocks
One important advantage is that the same Java test method can execute with different parameter values.
<suite name="LoginSuite">

    <test name="AdminLogin">
        <parameter name="username" value="admin"/>
        <parameter name="password" value="admin123"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="test.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="EmployeeLogin">
        <parameter name="username" value="employee"/>
        <parameter name="password" value="emp123"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="test.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>


Java class remains the same:
public class LoginTest {

    @Test
    @Parameters({"username", "password"})
    public void login(String username, String password) {

        System.out.println(username);
        System.out.println(password);

    }
}


Execution:
XML test block	Username	Password
AdminLogin	admin	admin123
EmployeeLogin	employee	emp123

The same test method will execute with different values for each XML <test> block.



3. Parameter scope
TestNG parameters can be defined at different XML levels.
XML level	Scope
<suite>	Available throughout the suite, unless overridden by a more specific definition.
<test>	Available within that XML <test> block.
<class>	Available to the relevant class.
<methods>	Available to the specified methods.

Example of suite-level parameter:
<suite name="Suite">

    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>

    <test name="Test1">
        <classes>
            <class name="test.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>


Java:
@Test
@Parameters("browser")
public void launchBrowser(String browser) {
    System.out.println(browser);
}


4. What happens if a parameter is missing?
Consider:
<test name="LoginTest">
    <parameter name="username" value="admin"/>
</test>


Java:
@Test
@Parameters({"username", "password"})
public void login(String username, String password) {
    System.out.println(username);
    System.out.println(password);
}


Here, password is not defined in XML.

Result: TestNG will report a parameter-related configuration error because the required parameter is missing.

You can handle missing parameters using @Optional.


5. @Optional annotation
@Optional provides a default value when an XML parameter is not supplied.
@Test
@Parameters({"username", "password"})
public void login(
        String username,
        @Optional("default123") String password) {

    System.out.println(username);
    System.out.println(password);
}


If password is not defined in XML, TestNG will use default123.

If the XML provides a password, the XML value will be used instead.

Remember: @Optional is useful when a parameter is not mandatory.



6. Using @Parameters with configuration annotations
You can also pass XML parameters to configuration methods.
@BeforeMethod
@Parameters("browser")
public void setup(String browser) {

    if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
    }
    else if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("firefox")) {
        driver = new FirefoxDriver();
    }

}


XML:
<test name="BrowserTest">
    <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>

    <classes>
        <class name="test.LoginTest"/>
    </classes>
</test>


This allows you to select the browser through XML without changing the Java code.



7. @Parameters vs @DataProvider
This is an important interview topic.
@Parameters	@DataProvider
Receives values from testng.xml.	Supplies data from a Java method.
Commonly used for configuration such as browser, environment, URL, etc.	Commonly used for data-driven testing.
Values are generally configured in XML.	Can provide multiple sets of test data.
Can pass values to configuration methods and test methods.	Primarily supplies data to test methods.


Example:

@Parameters:
@Test
@Parameters("browser")
public void test(String browser) {
    System.out.println(browser);
}


@DataProvider:
@DataProvider(name = "loginData")
public Object[][] getData() {

    return new Object[][] {
        {"admin", "admin123"},
        {"employee", "emp123"},
        {"manager", "manager123"}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "loginData")
public void login(String username, String password) {
    System.out.println(username + " " + password);
}


8. Important interview points
1. @Parameters is used to pass values from XML to Java methods.
2. Parameter names in Java must match the names defined in XML.
3. The order of method arguments must match the order specified in @Parameters.
4. The same test method can receive different values from different XML <test> blocks.
5. Missing mandatory parameters cause a parameter-related error.
6. @Optional provides a default value when an XML parameter is unavailable.
7. Parameters can be used with configuration methods such as @BeforeMethod.
8. A parameter defined at a narrower XML scope can override a broader-scope parameter with the same name.



9. Quick revision
Question	Answer
Which annotation passes XML values to Java?	@Parameters
Where are values defined?	testng.xml
What does name represent?	Parameter identifier
What does value represent?	Actual parameter value
What happens if a required parameter is missing?	Parameter-related error
How do you provide a default value?	@Optional
Can different XML <test> blocks pass different values?	Yes
Can it be used with @BeforeMethod?	Yes
Difference from DataProvider?	XML parameters vs Java-provided test data

Interview-ready answer:

"@Parameters is a TestNG annotation used to pass values from the testng.xml file to test methods or configuration methods. It supports parameterized execution, allowing the same test method to run with different values without modifying the Java code.

 If a parameter is optional, we can use the @Optional annotation to provide a default value."

-->
